Z||000005||Oreif||12-27-2000||01:12 PM||||As for installing scoop. If you use the IRM latch method, You need to make the baseplate to mount the scoop, and mounting plate inside the scoop to hold the strut. If you have a body shop do the mounting, I had estimates around $300. (Mounting and painting scoop) If you want to mount it like mine is, (scoop needs to be removed to open decklid/no restrictions on air coming in) You are looking at $75 to paint scoop and about $30 to prep the outside for painting (the scoop is far from "ready to paint") and cut the face open to make it functional. I spent $150 because I had it gel-coated. (gel-coat won't crack when handling the scoop) To make my mounting plate was $40.<P>The latch mounting is nice, but I've heard stories of the latch coming loose during driving and the scoop ripping off the car. The latch can be locked or pinned to prevent this. I chose the "remove scoop" method for cleaner install (didn't like the look of the big latch), stronger mounting, and maximum airflow thru the scoop.<BR>I can send detailed photos and drawings of my mounting for those interested in how I did it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Scoop_2.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||12-27-2000||01:40 PM||||i stayed a little closer to the method irm gives for mounting but without the strut or plates recommended for it inside. i kind of worked out a detatchable hinge (don't know if i can explain that. if you've worked with military test equipment with detatchable lids it's similar to that) with the latch holding down the front, pinned.<P> never painted mine cause i want the whole car painted eventually plus i want to put a third brake light in it when i find one and work out how to put it in in a way i like. probaly a long thin one that runs down the back. ||||reg||1
